TI  - Robotic lobectomy for non-small cell lung cancer: long-term oncologic results.
PG  - 157-62, vi
LID - S1547-4127(14)00012-7 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.thorsurg.2014.02.011 [doi]
AB  - Robotic lobectomy is a feasible, safe, and oncologically sound surgical treatment 
      of early-stage lung cancer. The technique is reproducible across multiple centers 
      and in the long-term yields results consistent with the best seen with 
      conventional video-assisted thoracic surgery (VATS) and thoracotomy. 
      Lymphadenectomy may reduce inadequate staging of the hilar and mediastinal nodes 
      during curative, anatomic resection. Differences between robotic versus VATS 
      versus thoracotomy approaches to thoracic diseases should be evaluated to define 
      the appropriate role of each approach.
