VI  - 2
DP  - 2016
TI  - How to deal with complicate hilar or interlobar lymphadenopathy during 
      video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery lobectomy.
PG  - 16
LID - 10.3978/j.issn.2221-2965.2016.01.01 [doi]
LID - 16
AB  - BACKGROUND: Lobectomy via video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) is steadily 
      becoming more commonplace. Concerns regarding oncologic effectiveness are focused 
      on the ability to achieve complete lymphadenectomy using VATS technique. 
      Effective VATS lymphadenectomy depends on a comprehensive understanding of the 
      anatomy. METHODS: In this report, we would provide comprehensive surgical 
      techniques on the management of complicate hilar or interlobar lymphadenectomy 
      via VATS. RESULTS: Two cases of VATS lymphadenectomy were shown in this study. 
      Various techniques have been adapted for lymphadenectomy with VATS. VATS 
      lymphadenectomy is equivalent to dissection via thoracotomy when performed by 
      experienced surgeons. Thoracic surgeons should keep in mind that to handle the 
      sophisticated lymph node (LN) situation could be a tedious and rough job. 
      CONCLUSIONS: Lymphadenectomy is a crucial element of staging for early stage 
      non-small cell lung cancer patients. During VATS lobectomy, lymphadenectomy must 
      be performed safely and effectively, with oncologic results comparable with those 
      of open procedures.
