VI  - 126
IP  - 14
DP  - 2013 Jul
TI  - Experience of segmentectomy from 36 Chinese patients with non-small cell lung 
      cancer at stage I.
PG  - 2687-93
AB  - BACKGROUND: Although video-assisted radical operation for lung cancer has been 
      widely accepted for treatment of nonsmall cell lung cancer (NSCLC), the debate 
      over video-assisted thoracic surgery (VATS) segmentectomy still remains. This 
      study analyzed the clinical outcomes using VATS segmentectomy for stage I NSCLC 
      patients to explore the safety and efficacy of VATS segmentectomy for Ia NSCLC. 
      METHODS: Retrospective review was conducted of patients who underwent VATS 
      segmentectomy for clinical stage I NSCLC at Shanghai Chest Hospital between 
      November 2009 and May 2012. VATS segmentectomy was performed on 36 patients. 
      Analyses of the patient group were performed on patient demographics and clinical 
      characteristics, intraoperative parameters, complications, and postoperative 
      survival. RESULTS: Thirty-five of thirty-six patients underwent VATS 
      segmentectomy with only one conversion to open thoracic surgery. There was one 
      peri-operative mortality from the segmentectomy group and all other patients are 
      alive with a median follow up of 327 days. The mean volume of chest tube drainage 
      after operation for segmentectomy was 1021.4 ml. Among other parameters, the mean 
      blood loss was 162.5 ml (50.0 - 1600.0 ml), the mean operation time 124.8 minutes 
      (75.0 - 271.0 minutes), chest tube duration 4.1 days (2 - 8 days), and the mean 
      length of hospital stay 6.2 days (4 - 11 days). There was one (2.8%) locoregional 
      recurrence after segmentectomy. Two patients successfully underwent bilateral 
      segmentectomies and are still disease free. CONCLUSION: For patients with stage I 
      NSCLC, VATS segmentectomy offers a safe and equally effective option and can be 
      applied to complicated operations such as bilateral segmentectomy.
