TI  - Preliminary experience with video-assisted thoracic surgery lobectomy for lung 
      malignancies: general considerations moving toward standard practice.
PG  - 43-6
LID - 10.2217/fon.15.305 [doi]
AB  - As part of the Second Catania Symposium on Thoracic Oncology, as we started the 
      experience with video-assisted thoracic surgery (VATS) lobectomy for lung 
      malignancies, we reviewed our data and argued some comments in a more general 
      discussion. Operated patients with non-small-cell lung cancer were divided in two 
      groups and compared: VATS (collected in a prospective database) and open 
      (historical group). Out of 74 patients, 31 in group A and 44 in group B. The 
      majority of patients in group A were stage I-II. Mean operative time was shorter 
      in group A. Postoperative hospital stay was shorter in group A. There was no 
      mortality. VATS is effective and safe to perform pulmonary lobectomy in our unit, 
      and it represents our preferred approach for early-stage lung cancer.
