DP  - 2008 Dec
TI  - Can stereotactic fractionated radiation therapy become the standard of care for 
      early stage non-small cell lung carcinoma.
PG  - 719-27
LID - 10.1016/j.ctrv.2008.06.001 [doi]
AB  - Management of early stages non-small cell lung carcinoma has traditionally been 
      surgery with radiation selected for medically inoperable patients. Recent 
      emergence of stereotactic body radiation therapy allows for delivery of high 
      radiation dose to the tumor while sparing normal lungs. This reviews compares 
      loco-regional control, survival and complications rates between these two 
      treatment modalities. Literature search identifies studies with stereotactic body 
      radiation therapy and surgery for early stages non-small cell lung cancer. 
      Effectiveness and toxicity of these two modalities was assessed. Stereotactic 
      body radiation therapy is associated with minimal morbidity and provides 
      comparable local control and survival in patients with multiple co-morbidities 
      factors. Stereotactic body radiation therapy may become standard of care for 
      operable early stages non-small cell lung cancer.
