IS  - 0021-5252 (Linking)
VI  - 76
IP  - 1
DP  - 2023 Jan
TI  - [Analysis of Surgical Margin Recurrence Following Segmentectomy for Early-stage 
      Non-small Cell Lung Cancer].
PG  - 84-89
AB  - BACKGROUND: Surgical margin recurrence following segmentectomy is a critical 
      issue because it may have been avoided by lobectomy. METHODS: Between January 
      2000 and December 2018, we retrospectively investigated 199 patients who 
      underwent segmentectomy for c-StageⅠ non-small cell lung cancer at our hospital. 
      RESULTS: Recurrence occurred in 20 cases, of which 3 cases had surgical margin 
      recurrence. In our previous study, the recurrence risk factor after segmentectomy 
      was radiologic solid tumor size( cut-off value 1.5 cm). Of the 130 patients in 
      the low-risk group with radiologic solid tumor size of less than 1.5 cm, five had 
      any recurrence, three of which had surgical margin recurrence. In the high-risk 
      group with radiologic solid tumor size of 1.5 cm or more, no surgical margin 
      recurrence was observed. Three cases of surgical margin recurrence were 
      accompanied by lepidic components, and the tumors were difficult to identify 
      intraoperatively and were located close to adjacent areas. CONCLUSION: Surgical 
      margin recurrence may be avoided by carefully considering the segments to be 
      resected and improving the method for identifying the intersegmental plane.
